PART 5 — APPROXIMATELY 45 SECONDS

So, what does procrastination actually cost us? A 1997 study by Tice and Baumeister followed university students across a semester. 
Students who procrastinated reported more stress and illness near the end, and received lower grades. 
The short-term relief came with a longer-term cost. For example, imagine leaving a group presentation until the night before. 
There is less time to check sources, practise speaking, or help your teammates. 
Even when the deadline is met, the group may have little time left to improve the work. So, what can we do?

