PART 6 — APPROXIMATELY 75 SECONDS

00:45–01:15 — TWO-MINUTE RULE
One approach is James Clear’s Two-Minute Rule: make the first step small enough to take less than two minutes. Instead of thinking, “I need to finish my whole presentation,” start by opening the document and writing one slide title. You have a clear first action, instead of facing the entire project at once. The aim is to make starting easier.

01:15–01:50 — POMODORO
Then try the Pomodoro Technique. Choose one task, focus for twenty-five minutes, and take a five-minute break. After four focus periods, take a longer break of fifteen to thirty minutes. For our presentation, one focus period could be used to prepare three key points, followed by a short break. The timer gives you a clear stopping point and a planned moment to rest. Then stand up and get some water.

01:50–02:00 — HANDOFF
Start with one small action, then focus. Next, we’ll look at turning our intentions into specific plans.

